  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-[#202020] dark:text-[#fcfcfc] mb-4 mt-8">
    Why Do You Get So Much Spam? The Root Causes
  </h2>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Before you can effectively stop spam, it is essential to understand how your email address ended up on spam distribution lists in the first place. Spammers rarely guess random email addresses out of thin air; instead, they rely on well-established harvesting pipelines:
  </p>

  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 mb-4">
    <li>
      <strong>Data Breaches and Leaks:</strong> Whenever an online store, forum, or SaaS service suffers a security breach, user databases containing millions of email addresses and passwords are leaked onto dark web forums and scraped by automated spam networks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Public Web Scraping:</strong> If your email address is visible on your personal portfolio, LinkedIn profile, GitHub commits, or public forums, automated bots index and add it to commercial mailing lists within minutes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data Brokers and Lead Resellers:</strong> Many free services, mobile apps, and sweepstakes websites sell user contact lists to third-party marketing brokers who bundle and resell that data to thousands of advertisers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dictionary and Brute-Force Attacks:</strong> Spammers generate millions of common prefix variations (e.g., <em>john.smith@</em>, <em>contact@</em>, <em>info@</em>) combined with popular domain names to find valid mailboxes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accidental Opt-Ins:</strong> Pre-checked consent boxes during checkout flows or account creations frequently enroll you into affiliate marketing networks without obvious notification.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#ea2804] pl-4 italic text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-400 mb-4">
