  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-[#202020] dark:text-[#fcfcfc] mb-4 mt-8">
    Smart Unsubscribe Rules: When to Click and When to Avoid
  </h2>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    When junk lands in your inbox, your first impulse might be to scroll down and immediately click the "Unsubscribe" link. However, doing this blindly can actually backfire dramatically depending on the sender.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold text-[#202020] dark:text-[#fcfcfc] mb-3 mt-6">
    When It Is Safe to Click Unsubscribe
  </h3>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Legitimate businesses, registered brands, and reputable SaaS platforms are legally bound by privacy regulations such as CAN-SPAM and GDPR. Their unsubscribe mechanisms are genuine and honor opt-out requests within a few business days. Use the native <strong>"Unsubscribe" button</strong> provided at the top of modern email clients (like Gmail or Apple Mail), which uses the standardized <code>List-Unsubscribe</code> header rather than an external web form.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold text-[#202020] dark:text-[#fcfcfc] mb-3 mt-6">
    When You Must NEVER Click Unsubscribe
  </h3>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    If the email comes from an unknown sender offering miracle health cures, dubious crypto investments, or unsolicited loan approvals, <strong>do not click any link inside the message</strong>, including "Unsubscribe" or "Remove me". Doing so provides the spammer with three critical confirmations:
  </p>

  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 mb-4">
    <li>It proves that your email address is active and monitored by a real human.</li>
    <li>It logs the exact time and device you used, increasing your value on spam reseller lists.</li>
    <li>It may trigger drive-by malware downloads or route you to a <a href="/blog/understanding-phishing-emails/" class="text-[#ea2804] hover:underline">phishing site designed to steal credentials</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    For these untrusted senders, the only correct action is to mark the message as spam/junk and delete it without opening links or downloading remote images.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-[#202020] dark:text-[#fcfcfc] mb-4 mt-8">
    Mastering Built-In Spam Filters & Custom Rules
  </h2>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Modern email providers utilize advanced machine learning algorithms and Bayesian filtering to categorize incoming mail. However, these systems require ongoing feedback to maintain peak accuracy:
  </p>

  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 mb-4">
    <li>
      <strong>Always "Report Spam" instead of deleting:</strong> Deleting an unsolicited email simply clears it from view, but clicking "Report Spam" feeds the message headers and sender fingerprints into your provider's Bayesian filter, training it to block similar messages for you and millions of other users.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rescue False Positives:</strong> Periodically review your Spam folder. If legitimate mail was misplaced, mark it as "Not Spam" to refine the filter's accuracy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Create Keyword and Domain Rules:</strong> Set automated filters for persistent nuisance patterns. For example, you can create a rule that automatically archives and marks as read any incoming message containing specific promotional keywords or coming from certain top-level domains (.xyz, .top, .icu) that you never legitimately interact with.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-[#202020] dark:text-[#fcfcfc] mb-4 mt-8">
    The Truth About "Plus Addressing" (+tag)
  </h2>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Many tech-savvy users rely on email subaddressing, such as entering <code>yourname+store@gmail.com</code> when signing up for services. While plus-addressing helps identify which company leaked or sold your address, it provides very limited spam protection:
  </p>

  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 mb-4">
    <li>Spammers use simple regex scripts to strip out the <code>+tag</code> portion before adding your base address to their database.</li>
    <li>Plus addressing still delivers all spam directly to your primary mailbox unless you manually configure complex filter rules for every single tag.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="text-base text-[#575757] dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
